md5sum: read the message little-endian on any host

MD5 defines its sixteen message words and the trailing bit count as
little-endian. md5.c cast the byte buffer to uint32_t * and copied the
length with memcpy, so it was correct only on a little-endian host: on a
big-endian one (a 68k or PowerPC Macintosh, OkapiaBench's targets) it hashes
other words, returns another digest, and verify_benchmark fails against
RESULT, which was computed on x86.

Assemble each word from its four bytes and write the length byte by byte.
The digest, and so RESULT, is unchanged on a little-endian host. A candidate
to send upstream (github.com/embench/embench-iot).

diff --git a/src/md5sum/md5.c b/src/md5sum/md5.c
index 092e183..ba91628 100644
--- a/src/md5sum/md5.c
+++ b/src/md5sum/md5.c
@@ -90,7 +90,10 @@ void md5(uint8_t *initial_msg, size_t initial_len) {
     msg[initial_len] = 128; // write the "1" bit
 
     uint32_t bits_len = 8*initial_len; // note, we append the len
-    memcpy(msg + new_len, &bits_len, 4);           // in bits at the end of the buffer
+    msg[new_len] = (uint8_t) bits_len;             // in bits at the end of the buffer,
+    msg[new_len + 1] = (uint8_t) (bits_len >> 8);  // little-endian as MD5 defines it
+    msg[new_len + 2] = (uint8_t) (bits_len >> 16); // whatever the host's byte order
+    msg[new_len + 3] = (uint8_t) (bits_len >> 24);
 
     // Process the message in successive 512-bit chunks:
     //for each 512-bit chunk of message:
@@ -98,7 +101,13 @@ void md5(uint8_t *initial_msg, size_t initial_len) {
     for(offset=0; offset<new_len; offset += (512/8)) {
 
         // break chunk into sixteen 32-bit words w[j], 0 ≤ j ≤ 15
-        uint32_t *w = (uint32_t *) (msg + offset);
+        uint32_t w[16];
+        int wi;
+        for (wi = 0; wi < 16; wi++)
+          w[wi] = (uint32_t) msg[offset + 4 * wi]
+                  | (uint32_t) msg[offset + 4 * wi + 1] << 8
+                  | (uint32_t) msg[offset + 4 * wi + 2] << 16
+                  | (uint32_t) msg[offset + 4 * wi + 3] << 24;
 
 #ifdef DEBUG
         printf("offset: %d %x\n", offset, offset);
